Sawdust Macabre

Ingredients: BRITISH BLUES & BLUES ROCK + COOL & WEST COAST JAZZ + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Brass  
Creative Catalyst: It was first performed at a clandestine, arsenic-laced wedding feast between two rival circus dynasties inside a rain-drowned big top.

Sawdust Macabre is the sound of cracked bone china and overdriven vacuum tubes played under an eclipse. It forces the frantic, sustain-heavy bends of late-60s London electric blues into the whispering, brushed polyrhythms and velvet counterpoint of Pacific seaboard cool jazz, then drowns the entire collision in cathedral plate reverb, funeral brass, and sulfurous dread. The brass does not swing—it prowls and mourns, issuing harmon-muted wails and guttural sousaphone drones beneath searing, distorted pentatonic leads.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 64–88 BPM. A languid, deceptive cool-jazz swing that drags heavily behind the beat, periodically ruptured by explosive, stomping 12/8 heavy blues-rock shuffles.
- Harmonic Palette: D minor and C minor blues scales grafted onto lush minor 9th and diminished major 7th West Coast chords, spiked with the tritone "devil’s interval" and chromatic circus-scale fanfares.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. The Funeral Horn Spill: Valve trombones and flugelhorns mic'd from forty paces down a subterranean marble tunnel, capturing decayed, ghostly room reflections.
  2. Saturated Valve Bleed: Overdriven Marshall JTM45 guitar amps bleeding directly into ribbon drum mics capturing whisper-quiet, wire-brushed ride cymbals.
  3. Arsenic Tape Warble: Nagra reel-to-reel master compression with manually introduced pitch flutter to mimic antique circus calliopes warping in the damp heat.
- Dynamics: Creeping, intimate whispers of brushed snare and isolated muted trumpet that suddenly detonate into deafening, fuzz-drenched heavy blues climaxes before evaporating back into icy, noir stillness.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre emerged from the "Carnival of the Two Scythes" in 1974, an unauthorized union between the aerialist dynasty Clan Vane and the lion-taming cartel House Malor beneath a rotting tent in Grimsby. When the dowry chest was discovered full of quicksilver and scorpions, the hired dance band—comprising expatriate Los Angeles jazz cool-cats and disgraced Soho blues shredders—refused to stop playing as knives were drawn. The resulting music became the ritual soundtrack for "The Low Wire," a nocturnal British subculture of chalk-faced buskers, velvet-clad carneys, and gothic blues purists. Adherents wear tailored Victorian undertaker coats over bare chests smeared with greasepaint, sporting lapels pinned with rusted brass trumpet mouthpieces. Their gatherings occur strictly in abandoned winter quarters and disused pier pavilions, preceded by the communal drinking of gin steeped in wormwood and the ritual burning of trapeze hemp.

Typical Instruments: Overdriven Gibson Les Paul (cranked through dying British valve heads), harmon-muted trumpet, valve trombone, flugelhorn, battered sousaphone, upright double bass bowed with wire, dry brushed jazz drum kit, and the "Callio-Scream" (a modified, steam-whistled pump organ wired through a Dallas Arbiter Fuzz Face).

For Fans Of: The Birthday Party, Morphine, Tom Waits, Peter Green’s Fleetwood Mac (late era), Bohren & der Club of Gore.

